module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.routing_a_subject_to_an_unverified_adjudicator

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data routing_a_subject_to_an_unverified_adjudicator: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "routing_a_subject_to_an_unverified_adjudicator" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**routing a subject to an adjudicator never observed to adjudicate it** (INVALID STATE: a subject whose adjudication is transferred to an authority that has never been OBSERVED to adjudicate it, with the transfer recorded as a ROUTING rather than as a loss of coverage. A gate that cannot reach a verdict on a subject declines it and names a second gate as better suited -- no per-item deadline, a larger budget, or ownership of the same subject universe -- and the naming is accepted as the coverage. The origin's decline is typed, located and honest; the destination's SILENCE is not, and nothing in the handing diff distinguishes a destination that answers from one that never runs. The subject then reads as adjudicated by everyone and is adjudicated by no one.",
    "WHY IT IS WORSE THAN THE RED IT REPLACES, which is the whole reason it is filed rather than treated as an ordinary oversight: the origin gate GOES GREEN on the decline. Before the routing the subject was LOUDLY unadjudicated -- a refusal somebody had to look at; after it, it is QUIETLY unadjudicated, and the quiet is indistinguishable from coverage by any instrument that reads the origin. It also INVERTS the DESIGN section 4b rung argument and borrows its authority: routing is defensible as a rung RAISE, from 'a lane that cannot reach a verdict' to 'a lane that can', and that argument is exactly what makes it attractive. But the raise is CONDITIONAL ON THE DESTINATION EXECUTING, and nobody checks the condition, because the reasoning has already satisfied them. A conditional rung claim whose condition is never read is section 4b(1) rung inflation wearing the costume of a climb.",
    "THE SPECIMEN, a near-miss on 2026-09-09/10 that was caught before anything was routed. Four witnesses of `v2.test.native_decl_selection` were refused INTERRUPTED-BEFORE-VERDICT at the floor's 500ms per-claim CPU ceiling, with claims_failed=0 -- UNDECIDED, not failing. The measured ladder: bare ingest of one four-line source ~725ms against eval_one ~24ms, so 97% of the cost was precondition rather than the claim. The ruling (quick-ibex-468) was that the floor DECLINE these four and that the `required-v2-native` job adjudicate them instead, on the reasoning that it owns the same `v2.test.*` universe and carries no per-witness deadline. THAT REASONING WAS SOUND AND THE CONCLUSION WAS WRONG, which is the shape of this class: the defect is not a bad argument, it is a good argument with an unread premise.",
    "WHAT THE DESTINATION ACTUALLY DID, checked by clever-dove-148 OUTSIDE ITS OWN SCOPE, which is the only reason it was found: `required-v2-native` refuses at its OWN emit/resolve stage before adjudicating ANY witness. Two completed runs, both failure -- one a `namespace_graft` type error, one an EmissionRefused on `00_compile.dag` -- with 4,999 of 5,371 modules unresolved. So the routing would have moved four witnesses to a lane that adjudicates NOTHING, and the floor would have been green over them. Nothing was routed: gunbc#10992 fixed the real defect by SHARING the fixture at the resolved seam so the ingest is paid once, and gunbc#11003 later deleted the `required-v2-native` job outright. Note the second fact carefully -- the destination named in the routing no longer exists, so the routed subjects would have been assigned to a deleted job by a decline that still read green.",
    "WHAT CAUGHT IT WAS NOT THE REASONING AND NOT REVIEW, and naming the wrong catcher is how this class gets mis-filed as a review-attention problem. It was an ACCEPTANCE CONDITION attached to the ruling: that `required-v2-native` must COMPLETE on the PR and NAME all four identities, and that its adjudication must not be INFERRED FROM A GREEN JOB. That condition is the repair this row points at, and it is a repair of exactly the right shape -- it demands the destination's own receipt at SUBJECT GRAIN, and refuses the two cheap substitutes for one (a green job, and an argument about the destination's capabilities). It is also a HUMAN discipline that executes nowhere and is enrolled nowhere, which is why the rung below is what it is.",
    "BOUNDARIES, stated because a row that does not discriminate is merged by a later reader, and every neighbour here shares the surface. NOT `shared_precondition_re_derived_once_per_claim_frame`, filed by clever-dove-148 over the same specimen. That class is about the COST being N copies of one derivation; this one is about where the SUBJECT GOES afterwards. The cost class is why the routing was TEMPTING; it is not the routing, and fixing it (as #10992 did) removes the temptation without removing this class. NOT `absorbing_fallback`: that arm WIDENS instead of refusing; this one does not widen at all -- it refuses correctly at the origin and hands the subject to a NAMED authority, and the defect is that the authority was never observed to answer. NOT an INERT LENS: an inert lens exists and is never consulted, whereas here the destination IS consulted and IS loud, failing at its own emit stage, and the LOUDNESS IS WHAT MAKES THE HANDOFF LOOK SERVICED. NOT an ESCAPE HATCH: an escape hatch proceeds as if the refusal had not fired, whereas here the refusal fires, is typed, and is located, and the subject is genuinely handed on. The failure lives at the RECEIVING end, which is precisely why it is invisible from the diff that does the handing.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: MITIGATABLE. The only thing standing between the routing and a silently uncovered subject was an acceptance condition written into a ruling by a human, executing nowhere and enrolled nowhere; a different reader, or the same reader on a busier night, routes it. CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, argued rather than asserted. A decline arm can be REQUIRED TO CITE A RECEIPT from the destination naming the routed subject, so a decline with no receipt REFUSES -- that is a decidable obligation over data the gate already produces, which is what puts it above mitigation. It is NOT structural impossibility and claiming so would be the inflation this file warns about: the invalid state stays AUTHORABLE, because a receipt can be STALE, or can name a run that completed without reaching a verdict on that subject, and no constructor can decide freshness or reachedness for a run it did not observe.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as the CAPABILITY and not as an artifact that would contribute to one (DESIGN section 4b(3)): a CROSS-GATE ADJUDICATION RECEIPT that a decline must cite, carrying the DESTINATION LANE, the RUN it was produced by, and the SUBJECT IDENTITIES that run actually reached a verdict on -- sufficient for a decline arm to REFUSE when the routed identity is absent from the cited run's verdict set. Anything less will be satisfied while the capability stays dead: a lane-name field is satisfied by naming a deleted job, a run-id field is satisfied by a run that refused at emit, and a green-job check is satisfied by exactly the inference the acceptance condition above forbids. The grain of the trigger must match the grain of the loss -- a per-SUBJECT loss demands a per-SUBJECT verdict set, never a per-lane status.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
